@meta
id: 07-13
title: Reading down a log half a screen at a time
chapter: 07
type: lesson
skills: scroll-half, screen-HML
par: 75
minutes: 8
vscode: the scrollbar, and losing your place twice
open: orders-service.log

@brief
An hour of log from the incident, 150 lines, pasted into the ticket. Four
of these lines are not `INFO`: three `ERROR` and one `WARN`. Mark each one
by putting `>>> ` at the front of it.

Nobody has told you which lines they are on. You have to read the file to
find them, and that is a different job from jumping to a place you already
know about. `G` and `47G` are no use when the whole task is finding out
where to go.

Do not use search — you have not been taught it yet, and this lesson is
about the thing you do before you know what to search for.

@keys
| `Ctrl-D` | down half a screen, taking the cursor with it |
| `Ctrl-U` | up half a screen |
| `j` `k` | the last line or two |
| `I` | insert at the front of the line |

@hints
You need to move the text past your eyes in readable chunks, not jump over
it. Half a screen at a time is the size that works, because half the screen
is still there afterwards and you keep your place.
--
`Ctrl-D` scrolls down half a screen and moves the cursor with it. `Ctrl-U`
does the same upwards. Pressing them repeatedly walks the whole file past
you at a pace you can actually read.
--
When a marked line comes into view, a couple of `j` or `k` presses land on
it, then `I` `>>> ` `Esc`. If a `Ctrl-D` takes you past one, `Ctrl-U` comes
straight back — overshooting and stepping back is normal and costs nothing.

@solution
One route, on a 24 line terminal. Yours will differ, because how far
`Ctrl-D` goes depends on how tall your window is, and that is the point of
it rather than a flaw.

    Ctrl-D x5   2j   I >>>  <Esc>
    Ctrl-D x6        I >>>  <Esc>
    Ctrl-D x5   2k   I >>>  <Esc>
    Ctrl-D x7   Ctrl-U   3j   I >>>  <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Fifty-eight keystrokes to read and annotate a hundred and fifty lines, and
most of them were one finger on Ctrl-D.

Half a screen is a deliberate choice and it is worth understanding why it
beats a full screen. After `Ctrl-D` the top half of your screen is the
bottom half of what you were just looking at, so your eye has an anchor and
you do not have to re-orient. Scroll a whole screen and every line is new,
which is why holding Page Down feels like being lost.

Two things people get wrong at first. The cursor comes with you, so you are
never scrolled away from where you are working — unlike a mouse wheel,
which moves the view and leaves the cursor behind, so your next keystroke
snaps you back somewhere you cannot see. And `Ctrl-U` is not undo. Undo is
`u`, it always has been, and `Ctrl-U` is the one that takes you back up the
file.

The other lesson here is about overshooting. A half screen is a coarse unit
and you will land past things. That is fine: coarse motion to get near,
then `j`, `k`, `H` or `L` for the last few lines. Trying to land exactly
with a scroll is the wrong kind of care.

